WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:02.410
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the
program today.

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:07.990
Bill Share, who is contributor to the Washington
Monthly and Real Clear Politics, also host

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:11.690
of the history podcast When America Worked.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.049
Bill, always great having you on.

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:14.589
Speaker 3: Always great to talk to you.

00:00:14.589 --> 00:00:22.019
Speaker 1: So I'm trying to understand different
individuals motivations in either being in

00:00:22.019 --> 00:00:28.779
favor of or against the various iterations
of the infrastructure proposals that we've

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:31.949
been seeing now for for several months.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:36.440
Let's kind of start with what's been more
in the news, which is maybe Joe Manchin, Joe

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.040
Manchin and Kirsten Cinema.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.920
When I step back and think about West Virginia,
you know, 47th in health care, very close

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.500
to 50th, if not 50th in education, very poor
infrastructure would be a place that would

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.670
really benefit from exactly this.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:59.170
It would seem like, hey, his constituents
would reward him for getting them some money.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:05.170
And then the story that's now floating around
is, well, look at his income from fossil fuel

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.560
companies that would be hurt by the climate
piece of this.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:09.560
Is it?

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.420
Is it as simple as a follow the money type
thing?

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:16.950
Speaker 3: Well, I don't want to play mind
reader, and I understand people wanting to

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.310
connect dots, and everyone's welcome to have
their own theories.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:21.390
Sure.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:28.090
But you people went into this year thinking,
Oh, Manchin likes the posture, but this is

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:31.580
essentially a populous state, and so he'll
do the populist thing.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.880
Yeah, and now he's not doing the populist
thing.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.870
So they're coming up with fresh theories.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:40.080
I think you look at Manchin's longer record
in politics from his very early days in West

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.080
Virginia.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:46.380
He has always been a business friendly centrist
that has always been his thing.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:47.890
It's not new.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.030
And why he's a business really centrist.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.450
You're welcome to have your own theories about
that, but that just seems to be the case.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:55.450
And.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:01.860
Are you at the end of the day, if you want
this bill to pass, you need his vote.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.840
However annoying it is to get that vote, it
is needed.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.440
And I think this is also true for cinema.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:14.170
I think the strategy of brow beating them
publicly.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:19.459
And challenging their political acumen, accusing
them of corruption.

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:23.639
It may all be true, but it's not getting you
their vote.

00:02:23.639 --> 00:02:26.719
They're not being shamed into moving off their
position.

00:02:26.719 --> 00:02:29.719
So I think a different tack needs to be taken.

00:02:29.719 --> 00:02:34.239
If they're if you're going to get to 50, which
may be happening, that we're seeing the conversation

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:35.239
is starting to materialize now.

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:41.660
Speaker 1: Yeah, you and I both, I think,
tweeted a disfavor about following Kirsten

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.549
Cinema into the bathroom.

00:02:44.549 --> 00:02:50.799
And as I said, you know what I said on my
show was maybe you could make the case that

00:02:50.799 --> 00:02:55.810
the bathroom is the only place where she might
be confronted by an opposing view.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:03.409
It seems contrary to reality, and thus that
is a tactic doesn't seem to me to be particularly

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:04.409
productive.

00:03:04.409 --> 00:03:05.409
But what?

00:03:05.409 --> 00:03:11.469
What can be done to effectively pressure a
lot of these folks who, you know, the voters

00:03:11.469 --> 00:03:18.069
swam up to the yacht of Joe Manchin, and it
wasn't particularly inspiring the things that

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:22.129
he said to me like what actually works to
pressure these people from from an individual

00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:25.510
voter standpoint,
Speaker 3: you know, different tactics work,

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:26.510
different people.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:31.980
There's not a one size fits all here, but
at minimum, for the last several weeks of

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.900
months, people have tried confrontation.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.629
People have tried give you their personal
spaces.

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:42.269
People have tried haranguing them as being
on the take.

00:03:42.269 --> 00:03:46.799
And not only have they not moved, they seem
to have, you know, dug in their heels.

00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:52.819
And even at the precise moment when progressives
thought they were gaining more leverage by

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:59.310
blocking the the bipartisan infrastructure
bill that Joe Manchin spearheaded, Sinema

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.749
essentially wrote It's called cinema Portman,
for Pete's sake.

00:04:02.749 --> 00:04:06.830
Yeah, thinking, Well, we hold that hostage,
that what's prize to them?

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:11.069
They'll have to come up to come around almost
immediately.

00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:15.999
That price tag on build back better went down
by nearly 50 percent.

00:04:15.999 --> 00:04:16.999
Right.

00:04:16.999 --> 00:04:19.199
So the tactics just aren't working.

00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:25.990
And so I just think the entire approach of
treating them as effectively adversaries,

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:30.510
people who are not progressive, who don't
see the world the way we do and need to be

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.900
dragged in doing the right thing.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.770
That approach isn't working.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:37.150
I think you want to get the temperature down.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:41.110
You want to get into the same room with them
and try to talk to them and figure out where

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.110
is the common ground?

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:43.110
I mean, there's no common.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.110
There's no common ground.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.250
I think there is some common ground.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:50.990
You got to find where that is and not make
it into a grudge match because a grudge match

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:52.440
isn't working in progressives favor.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.240
Speaker 1: Yeah, I do think that part of what
makes it difficult is it seems as though Joe

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.759
Manchin is very dishonest.

00:04:58.759 --> 00:05:02.960
And what I mean by that is for a while, he
was saying the problem was the price tag.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.300
As you mentioned, iterations of this have
seen a much reduced price tag.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.770
And now he doesn't like it for different reasons.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:14.300
So part of it is maybe, maybe common ground
is the way to describe that.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.259
But also moving goalposts seems to be a real
problem here.

00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:20.790
Speaker 3: Well, both Manchin and cinema can
be.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:25.639
I mean, Manchin can use a lot of word salad,
which makes him inscrutable in certain ways.

00:05:25.639 --> 00:05:29.250
And cinema is very quiet, which makes her
inscrutable.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.430
And this frustrates the hell out of progressives.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:35.610
I think it's a negotiating tactic is working
very well for them, and everyone's chasing

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:36.610
their votes, right?

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.620
Throwing things at them to figure what's going
to make them happy.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:41.669
So they're in the driver's seat.

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:48.080
I don't think it's fair to say Manchin has
moved the goalpost because he has been consistent

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.080
on a number of things.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:53.440
You look at that piece of paper that he signed
with Chuck Schumer back in July.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.080
Now, granted, this wasn't known to the rest
of us at the time, but he communicated this

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.550
to his majority leader of all the things that
he wanted that he was comfortable with.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:05.629
And essentially by omission what he was not
comfortable with.

00:06:05.629 --> 00:06:06.949
That's all been pretty consistent.

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:08.180
He's been saying for a long time.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:13.060
He doesn't like this clean energy payment
program, which was written to be the centerpiece

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:15.199
of the climate section of the bill.

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:17.210
That's not new.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:24.650
So I think he's always had a combination of
price tag concerns and substantive concerns,

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:32.050
and people have tried to grind him down instead
of accepting that he holds a determining vote.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:33.750
And so what are his concerns?

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.099
De facto become your concerns.

00:06:35.099 --> 00:06:37.319
You need to resolve those concerns to get
a bill.

00:06:37.319 --> 00:06:43.439
Speaker 1: There is many progressives who
every time this happens, say we got a primary

00:06:43.439 --> 00:06:49.439
Joe Manchin, and whenever that happens, I
tend to think, Well, I don't know that if

00:06:49.439 --> 00:06:55.610
a further left Democrat wins the primary,
they actually win the general Senate election

00:06:55.610 --> 00:07:00.250
in West Virginia that might lead to replacing
a Democrat with a Republican.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.639
I think we need to be solving this.

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:09.539
You solve 50 50 by taking some existing Republican
seats and turning them to Democratic seats.

00:07:09.539 --> 00:07:13.340
What's your general perspective on this idea
of Prime?

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:18.129
Marrying Joe Manchin, even as a pressure tactic
to get him to be scared in some way.

00:07:18.129 --> 00:07:21.060
Speaker 3: Well, he's been primaried and he's
unfazed.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.250
So I mean, West Virginia is just too red.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:27.050
The fact that Joe Manchin is there at all
is a gift for Democrats.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:28.050
They should be.

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:30.379
They will not have a majority without Joe
Manchin right now.

00:07:30.379 --> 00:07:34.500
It would be Mitch McConnell, and we would
be talking about a multitrillion dollar bill

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:35.729
at all.

00:07:35.729 --> 00:07:39.249
So Manchin shrugs that off very gleefully.

00:07:39.249 --> 00:07:41.879
Now, cinema is a different story.

00:07:41.879 --> 00:07:43.289
Yes.

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:48.759
Cinema seems to have a theory of the case
that she doesn't care, either if she gets

00:07:48.759 --> 00:07:53.280
primaried, that her path of success is, you
know, is mavericky.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:08:01.479
A. McCain like independence from party leadership,
and even though those primary threats are

00:08:01.479 --> 00:08:07.580
more pronounced and are very vocalized right
now, again, she is not budging.

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:12.889
So I understand worth trying it out to see
if it might get her running scared, but she's

00:08:12.889 --> 00:08:13.979
not running scared.

00:08:13.979 --> 00:08:17.120
So, I mean, it may be by 2024, which is up.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:18.120
She'll lose that primary.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.740
I can't say I think she will probably survive
a primary.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:26.680
It's very unusual for an incumbent to be ousted
in a primary, particularly ideological reasons.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:30.189
Usually, if it happens, it's for a scandal
reason.

00:08:30.189 --> 00:08:33.419
So Arizona is still pretty purple.

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:37.870
It's not hard blue, and there are a lot of
people in Arizona even who are Democrats who

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.080
say electability comes first.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:46.560
So I would still put my money on cinema surviving,
but it's certainly not a slam dunk.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:50.810
But nevertheless, she is not being cowed by
that pressure tactic.

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:54.830
Speaker 1: What is going to or less will work
backwards?

00:08:54.830 --> 00:09:00.650
Does this bill's passage or failure affect
Democrats in 2022?

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:05.370
Is it the type of thing that voters would
actually vote one way or the other on?

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:10.600
Does Joe Biden getting something else big
done impact Democrats in 2022?

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.130
What does matter looking at the midterms?

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:14.890
Speaker 3: Well, there's two ways to look
at 2022.

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:17.450
One is Democrats are going to lose no matter
what.

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:20.950
The midterm princess party, they always lose
something.

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:25.770
The margins are so narrow that just the tiniest
tiniest bit of something means it's all over.

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:27.240
So get what you can.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:30.200
While you can even worry about the politics,
right?

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.780
One school of thought not illegitimate.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:37.590
The other is, you know, even though it doesn't
usually happen, it can happen.

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:40.130
It's happened three times in the past.

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:44.830
You know, I think post-reconstruction that
the president's party has gained a few seats

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:50.150
and I think a fourth time when they just lost
a very, very little enough that Democrats

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:53.440
could actually survive this time around.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:59.330
And when those things happen, it's almost
always because there's some kind of crisis.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:04.520
And the president's party is seen as managing
that crisis as well.

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:10.400
And so the usual dynamic of the president's
party's coalition splintering because of the

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:16.390
natural realities of governing trade offs
compromises imperfections are unfulfilled

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:21.040
aspirations and dreams, things that deplete
some of your base or make some people the

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:25.430
middle peel off while your opposition is unified
against whatever it is the president's party

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:26.430
is doing.

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:30.370
That's the reason why the president's party
usually loses.

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:36.270
So if Democrats can't pass, build back better
at all, or if it's reduced to such a nub that

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:41.210
it makes people some people unhappy, that
makes some people on the base displeased.

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:46.680
You know, even if it's a good general election
politics, it's good for the center if that

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:47.680
base is unhappy.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:52.620
You know, if any, if any faction is unhappy,
that's bad for the midterms.

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:57.480
You need full unity to overcome the historical
trend.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.970
So that's another reason why it's not good
for Democrats to be in this tug of war right

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:05.740
now, where someone's going to feel bad regardless
of the outcome.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:10.030
You want everyone to feel good about the outcome,
even though it's almost certainly going to

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:11.190
be some kind of compromise.

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:17.990
Speaker 1: What about something like the Afghanistan
withdrawal, which, by the way, I was and remain

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:22.080
completely in favor of and believe it would
have been just as if not more chaotic under

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:27.030
Donald Trump, but that ultimately it's the
right decision when that took place in August

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:28.450
into September.

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:34.070
There was a lot of talk of this will torpedo
the midterms for Democrats, and I really didn't

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:35.910
see it for two reasons.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:38.170
One, that it really was a Biden thing.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.450
And of course, it can affect the midterms.

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:41.950
We're talking about that.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:45.090
But number two, it just seems like it.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:50.490
There is enough time between then and the
midterms and the relatively short memories

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:54.290
of voters struck me as I don't think Afghanistan
is going to play a factor in the midterms.

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:55.290
What do you think?

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:59.470
Speaker 3: I mean, I wrote a piece to that
effect at the Washington Monthly at the time

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:04.880
when I said with the caveat of some kind of
9-11 occurring, some kind of terrorist attack

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:10.280
on our soil launched from Afghanistan because
it turns out that was able to regroup under

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:11.440
the current circumstances.

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.740
That would be, you know, obviously.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:18.270
Bad for people's lives and bad for Democrats
politically.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.840
Outside of that, I think we're already seeing
Afghanistan fading into the background of

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.710
politics.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:25.820
Having said that?

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:34.340
Biden has not had a single day with a good
headline since early August when the Afghanistan

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:35.340
withdrawal occurred.

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:40.790
And if it's Afghanistan, it's COVID Delta
variant, it's not COVID, it's something on

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:44.480
the border, but it's saying the border is
the infrastructure bill being blocked, the

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:46.160
infrastructure bill being blocked.

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:49.760
It's that build back better is in limbo or
there's a debt limit drama.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:51.260
You know, it's always something.

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:54.340
Yeah, and there hasn't been a good day.

00:12:54.340 --> 00:13:00.780
And Afghanistan was just the first time when
there was an obviously bad day or people sort

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:03.550
of saying, Wait a second, maybe these guys
don't know what they're doing.

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:04.580
Maybe they're not perfect.

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:07.630
Maybe they're not the most competent operation
in the universe.

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:12.310
You can fix that with some wins, but putting
some points on the board, which is why I wonder.

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:16.330
I mean, I can't I can't predict this, and
it's not the way it panned out.

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:20.480
You know, two and half weeks ago, but we are
coming up on another deadline on October 30,

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:28.810
first where Nancy Pelosi said we need to pass
the bipartisan infrastructure bill by this

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:33.250
date because the Highway Trust Fund is going
to expire now.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:36.840
She said the same thing a month ago, and what
happened a month ago was that Progressive

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:38.310
said, We don't care.

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:39.600
We're not going to give you the votes.

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:44.650
Biden came to the House and said, Look, this
has to be done at the same time.

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:50.610
And then people scrambled and pass the Highway
Trust Fund extension for 30 days, and Progressive

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:54.890
essentially said this isn't a real deadline
because you can push that off.

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:56.520
They were proven correct.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:14:00.750
However, you can only push off that highway
trust fund deadline if Republicans in the

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:01.860
Senate agree.

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:02.860
That's not.

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:03.860
It's not a reconciliation vote.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:05.910
That's a regular order vote.

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:09.420
Republicans did that last month because they
said this isn't.

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:10.880
This isn't costing the extra money.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:16.340
There's enough money in the kitty for the
provincial government to pay out what's needed

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:19.920
for road and transit projects for states and
localities.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:23.290
But that may not be the case two weeks from
now.

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:25.010
So it's a real hard deadline, I should say.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:29.140
One of their negative headline the past two
weeks has been the supply chain issue.

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:33.060
Yes, Biden is saying he's trying to fix that.

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:34.870
He knows it's a serious problem.

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:39.000
And over the long haul, we need to pass the
bipartisan infrastructure bill because it

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.940
